WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Welcome to today's explainer. We are jumping right into what is easily one of the most fascinating,

00:05.000 --> 00:11.000
and honestly most misunderstood, topics in tech right now. How generative AI systems actually process

00:11.000 --> 00:16.000
and retain information. Look, we all interact with these tools every single day, but there's a ton of confusion

00:16.000 --> 00:22.000
about what's actually happening under the hood. Does the AI learn from every conversation we have with it?

00:22.000 --> 00:28.000
Does it secretly keep a giant memory bank of facts? Well, today we're going beyond the prompt to decode the reality

00:28.000 --> 00:35.000
of how AI really learns. Here's our roadmap for the explainer today. First, models versus complete applications.

00:35.000 --> 00:42.000
Second, training updates the parameters. Third, inference with fixed models. Fourth, retrieval-augmented generation,

00:42.000 --> 00:47.000
or RAG, and finally, reliability and limitations. Okay, let's kick things off with a question

00:47.000 --> 00:52.000
that gets right to the heart of a massive misconception. If you type in this chapter,

00:52.000 --> 00:59.000
X means Y into a prompt. Does the model permanently learn that fact? Does it update its internal weights?

00:59.000 --> 01:04.000
Take a second to really think about your answer. You know, a lot of people just assume that because the AI uses

01:04.000 --> 01:09.000
that new info in its very next reply, its brain must have been permanently rewired.

01:09.000 --> 01:15.000
But as we're going to see, the reality of how these systems hold on to information is totally different from human memory.

01:15.000 --> 01:20.000
Before we get into the heavy mechanics, we need to establish a baseline vocabulary.

01:20.000 --> 01:27.000
What exactly is the engine driving all of this? At its core, a generative language model is an auto-regressive system.

01:27.000 --> 01:32.000
Now, all that fancy term means is that it assigns probabilities to token sequences.

01:32.000 --> 01:39.000
It looks at the context it already has and calculates which token, well, which word, part of a word, or even punctuation mark,

01:39.000 --> 01:42.000
might logically come next. That is literally it.

01:42.000 --> 01:47.000
Repeating that specific calculation over and over again is what builds out a response.

01:47.000 --> 01:51.000
It's a probability engine, folks. Not a conscious fact-checker.

01:51.000 --> 01:58.000
Models versus complete applications. So there is a hard, absolute boundary we have to draw between the core model itself

01:58.000 --> 02:04.000
and the software wrapped around it. The core model simply calculates token probabilities based on context.

02:04.000 --> 02:09.000
That's the engine. But the complete application, like the slick chat interfaces we use daily,

02:09.000 --> 02:15.000
that adds system instructions, web search capabilities, file retrieval, filters, and managed memory.

02:15.000 --> 02:21.000
The fluent human-sounding writing? That comes from the core model. But pulling up a PDF or browsing the web,

02:21.000 --> 02:27.000
that entirely belongs to the surrounding application. Training updates the parameters.

02:27.000 --> 02:32.000
This definitive line is absolutely crucial when we look at different ways we intervene with the AI.

02:32.000 --> 02:39.000
If you want to permanently change the model's core numerical values, you have to actually train or fine-tune it.

02:39.000 --> 02:44.000
Just adding a new message in the context window or uploading an external document only changes the information available

02:44.000 --> 02:51.000
for that one specific calculation. It does not, in any way, update the permanent weights of the model.

02:51.000 --> 02:57.000
So what does actual training look like? It's a highly mechanical, computationally heavy flow.

02:57.000 --> 03:03.000
First, massive amounts of text examples are fed into the system. The system tries to predict the next tokens,

03:03.000 --> 03:06.000
compares them to the actual text, and calculates its prediction error.

03:07.000 --> 03:14.000
Next, these intense optimization algorithms execute physical numerical updates to the parameter values to reduce that aggregate error.

03:14.000 --> 03:19.000
The end result is a permanently changed model. The text just supplies the learning signal.

03:19.000 --> 03:27.000
The mathematical optimization does the actual rewiring. It's serious heavy lifting, and it's completely separate from you just typing a prompt into a chat box.

03:27.000 --> 03:30.000
Inference with fixed models.

03:30.000 --> 03:34.000
Now contrast all that heavy lifting with ordinary inference.

03:34.000 --> 03:39.000
When you send a prompt, the model is completely frozen. Its parameters do not change.

03:39.000 --> 03:47.000
It takes your input, runs calculations based purely on that prompt and its frozen parameters, and creates a probability distribution for the next token.

03:47.000 --> 03:54.000
Finally, an output is selected. Keeping the model fixed while changing your input is really just exploring the effective context.

03:54.000 --> 03:59.000
You aren't teaching the AI anything permanent. You're just giving it different starting conditions for its math.

03:59.000 --> 04:05.000
Let's actually ground this math in reality with a recorded run from a real model, Qwen 2.5.

04:05.000 --> 04:11.000
Given the context, the capital of Italy is. The model calculates continuation probabilities.

04:11.000 --> 04:16.000
As you can see, the token for Rome gets the highest probability at 44.86%.

04:16.000 --> 04:20.000
Located gets 10.35% and so on.

04:20.000 --> 04:25.000
Notice that these values are continuation probabilities, not absolute truth scores.

04:25.000 --> 04:29.000
The model doesn't know Rome is the capital in the way you or I do.

04:29.000 --> 04:36.000
It merely calculated that Rome is the most probable next token based entirely on its fixed parameters and the context you gave it.

04:36.000 --> 04:39.000
Retrieval Augmented Generation, or RAG.

04:39.000 --> 04:48.000
So if a fixed model can't learn new facts on the fly, how in the world do we get it to answer questions about a brand new document, or today's news?

04:48.000 --> 04:50.000
Well, we use RAG.

04:50.000 --> 04:52.000
Think of it as an open book test for the AI.

04:52.000 --> 05:00.000
You ask a question, and a separate search component goes out and retrieves relevant passages from an external database, like a PDF you just uploaded.

05:00.000 --> 05:07.000
Those passages are stuffed right into the context window alongside your question and handed over to the fixed model to read.

05:07.000 --> 05:13.000
The model then generates an answer based purely on that newly provided temporary context.

05:13.000 --> 05:15.000
Let's make this crystal clear.

05:15.000 --> 05:20.000
RAG searches external documents and makes those passages available to the generation context.

05:20.000 --> 05:24.000
Crucially, it does not change the model's parameters or weights.

05:24.000 --> 05:31.000
Uploading a PDF just updates the available information for that one conversation, not the AI's permanent brain.

05:31.000 --> 05:32.000
And remember the limits here.

05:32.000 --> 05:34.000
The retrieval part can fail.

05:34.000 --> 05:36.000
It can grab completely irrelevant data.

05:36.000 --> 05:40.000
Or the model might just misinterpret the material you supplied it entirely.

05:40.000 --> 05:42.000
Reliability and Limitations.

05:42.000 --> 05:48.000
It is incredibly important to remember what we've unpacked today when you're evaluating AI outputs.

05:48.000 --> 05:53.000
A model can easily produce plausible but totally incorrect text, what we call hallucinations.

05:53.000 --> 05:56.000
There isn't just one single cause for this.

05:56.000 --> 06:01.000
And lowering the model's temperature or creativity settings isn't a magic fix.

06:01.000 --> 06:04.000
Think back to that Qwen model predicting Rome.

06:04.000 --> 06:12.000
Even when it produces a highly confident token probability, it is still just predicting the next word based on frozen parameters.

06:12.000 --> 06:18.000
An 80% probability for a token absolutely does not mean the claim has an 80% chance of being factually true.

06:18.000 --> 06:22.000
I want to leave you with this final really important thought from our source material.

06:22.000 --> 06:25.000
A link is not automatic proof.

06:25.000 --> 06:31.000
If the text does not contain the answer, the student should recognize that limit and seek another appropriate source.

06:31.000 --> 06:37.000
Because generative AI is ultimately a probability engine, running fixed parameters on the context it's given.

06:37.000 --> 06:40.000
Human verification remains absolutely essential.

06:40.000 --> 06:44.000
Fluent writing simply doesn't equal factual accuracy.

06:44.000 --> 06:47.000
So the next time you use an AI tool, ask yourself this.

06:47.000 --> 06:51.000
Are you trusting the machine's frozen math or the context you actually provided?

06:51.000 --> 06:54.000
Thanks so much for joining me for this explainer.

